1. She is half Asian and half English. (Verbatim: "I'm half Asian. And I'm half English.")
2. Her mother is fully Asian and mixed (with Chinese heritage), while her father is British. (Verbatim: "My father's British and my mother— This is why I keep it simple, guys. This is why I just say I'm half Asian.")
3. She was born in Germany but does not identify as German and holds a British passport. (Verbatim: "I was born in Germany... I have a British passport. I'm not half German, I'm not German at all actually.")
4. She was raised in England after moving there from Germany. (Verbatim: "And thus, raised in England.")
5. She cannot speak or understand German despite being born there. (Verbatim: "And thus, I don't know any German, I'm not even going to pretend.")
6. Her English ancestry includes Irish descent. (Verbatim: "My English side descends from Ireland.")
7. She struggled to learn Polish and was only taught insults by her Polish friends. (Verbatim: "When my Polish friends tried to teach me Polish, they said I sound like a Chinese person trying to speak English... they only taught me really rude things. Like, insults.")
8. She studied Mandarin in school instead of Latin during her final year. (Verbatim: "For the final year, they were like, ooh, you can choose... Latin or Mandarin, and I was like, heck yeah, Mandarin.")
9. She finds tonal languages difficult and struggles with accents, especially those involving tone. (Verbatim: "Wait, yeah, tonal is difficult. Um, anything with tone I struggle with.")
10. She is a fan of isekai anime and manhwa, particularly those involving time regression. (Verbatim: "Isekai is my anime junk food... I'm reading something like that, I'm reading like a manhwa about the world's greatest swordsman regression.") [end of text]



1. She is a fully trained data scientist with experience in R and statistical analysis. (Verbatim: "i am a fully trained data scientist no r and everything etc get a get a job")

2. She previously worked in third-line IT support at a tech company that heavily relied on outdated systems like Excel and VBA. (Verbatim: "i was doing third line support at this company... this entire company i was working for uses is still like... behind it was how many years")

3. She has professional experience with Jira and finds ticketing systems painful but likes to keep her own tickets organized. (Verbatim: "anyone else use Jira oh Jira tickets pain tickets... I like to keep my tickets tidy")

4. She studied data science at university, partly to satisfy her parents' concern about job security. (Verbatim: "my parents basically wanted to make sure i wouldn't ever be jobless um so i i am a i am a fully trained data scientist")

5. She dislikes R as a programming language despite being trained in it, finding it counterintuitive. (Verbatim: "r is um is painful painful... it's still counterintuitive")

6. She prefers Python for data analysis because she likes the idea of using pandas. (Verbatim: "python with pandas sounds fun i actually only wanted to start doing my statistical analyses... because i like the idea of it being pandas")

7. She has a strong work ethic instilled from a young age but later reevaluated it by asking "what's the risk?" to reduce unnecessary stress. (Verbatim: "I was like raised with a really strong work ethic... then I realized wait I gotta take a step back and ask myself why why")

8. She once worked a job where she was expected to do the work of three people without a corresponding increase in pay. (Verbatim: "now i have to do the job of three people but i'm still being paid the job of one person so why don't you times three my salary")

9. She left a job because the company tried to exploit her by overloading responsibilities without fair compensation. (Verbatim: "i straight up was like i'm going to leave... i don't want to do i mean i can do it... but i don't want to for this pay")

10. She has experience remotely accessing and supporting legacy systems like Windows XP and Windows 98. (Verbatim: "dude i used to do it support and have to remote onto these servers that we're still using like windows 98 or windows xp") [end of text]



